This peer constructs the least fixed-point operation on endomaps, proves that it preserves finitary projections under a natural closure hypothesis, and identifies the resulting fixed domains.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`FixOp.eq_bot_of_fixed_constMap_bot`).** The only element fixed by the constant map with value bottom is bottom itself.

*Proof.* The image of every element under the constant map is bottom, so the fixed-point equation immediately identifies the element with bottom. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.uniqueFixedConstMapBot`).** The fixed-point type of the constant-bottom map has exactly one element.

*Proof.* Bottom is fixed, and every fixed element is bottom by the preceding fact. ∎

**Definition (`FixOp.finitaryWitnessEquivBot`).** The fixed-point poset of the constant-bottom map is order-isomorphic to the one-element neighbourhood system.

*Proof.* Both posets have a unique element, so the unique maps in both directions are mutually inverse and preserve the order. ∎

**Definition (`FixOp.fixOp`).** For an endomap \(t\) of the function-space domain \([E\to E]\), its fixed-point operator is the approximable endomap of \(E\) represented by the least fixed element of \(t\).

*Proof.* Convert the least fixed element of \(t\) from a function-space element to its corresponding approximable map. ∎

**Definition (`FixOp.tOp`).** An operator \(t\) on endomaps acts on an endomap \(a\) by applying \(t\) to the function-space element represented by \(a\), then converting the result back to an approximable map.

*Proof.* This is the transport of the element action of \(t\) across the correspondence between function-space elements and approximable maps. ∎

**Definition (`FixOp.Fix`).** For an endomap \(a:E\to E\), \(\operatorname{Fix}(a)\) is the ordered type of elements \(y\) satisfying \(a(y)=y\).

*Proof.* Take the subtype of the element poset cut out by the fixed-point equation, with its inherited order. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.constMap_bot_le_idMap`).** The constant-bottom endomap lies below the identity endomap.

*Proof.* Every value of the constant map is bottom, and bottom lies below every element. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.isRetraction_constMap_bot`).** The constant-bottom endomap is idempotent.

*Proof.* Applying the map once or twice always returns bottom. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.isProjection_constMap_bot`).** The constant-bottom endomap is a projection.

*Proof.* It is idempotent and lies below the identity. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.isFinitaryProjection_constMap_bot`).** The constant-bottom endomap is a finitary projection.

*Proof.* Its fixed-point poset is order-isomorphic to the one-element neighbourhood system, which supplies a finite witness for the projection. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.iterElem_succ'`).** The \((n+1)\)-st approximation in the iteration of \(t\) is obtained by applying \(t\) to the \(n\)-th approximation.

*Proof.* Successor iteration is composition with \(t\), and element maps carry composition to successive application. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.iterElem_zero'`).** The zeroth approximation in the iteration of \(t\) is the bottom element of the function-space domain.

*Proof.* Zeroth iteration is the identity, evaluated at bottom. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.rel_master_of_mem`).** If \(X\) is a neighbourhood of \(E\), then every approximable endomap relates \(X\) to the master neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Start with the map's master relation and use monotonicity, since \(X\) is contained in the master neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.tOp_fixOp`).** The endomap obtained as the least fixed point of \(t\) is fixed by the induced action of \(t\).

*Proof.* The least fixed element of \(t\) is fixed by \(t\), and the element–map correspondence preserves this equation. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.tOp_toApproxMap`).** Acting on the approximable map represented by a function-space element \(\varphi\) yields the approximable map represented by \(t(\varphi)\).

*Proof.* The two conversions between function-space elements and approximable maps are inverse. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.toApproxMap_bot_eq_constMap`).** The approximable map represented by the bottom function-space element is the constant-bottom map.

*Proof.* A relation belongs to the bottom function exactly when its input is a neighbourhood and its output is bottom; this is precisely the relation of the constant-bottom map. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.isFinitaryProjection_fixOp`).** Suppose the action induced by \(t\) sends every finitary projection to a finitary projection. Then the least fixed-point endomap of \(t\) is itself a finitary projection.

*Proof.* Beginning with the constant-bottom finitary projection, induction shows that every finite iterate is finitary. The least fixed element is the directed supremum of these iterates, and the finitary core operation fixes each iterate and commutes with this directed supremum; hence it fixes the limit, which characterizes a finitary projection. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.fixedDomain_fixOp_iso_T`).** Suppose the induced action of \(t\) preserves finitary projections, and the fixed domain of its value at each finitary projection \(a\) is order-isomorphic to a specified domain \(T(a)\). Then the fixed domain of the least fixed-point endomap is order-isomorphic to \(T\) evaluated at that endomap.

*Proof.* The least fixed-point endomap is finitary and is fixed by the induced action, so specialize the assumed family of order isomorphisms to it. ∎

**Theorem (`FixOp.fixedNbhd_fixOp_subsystem`).** Under the same preservation hypothesis, if \(a\) is a finitary projection and the induced action sends \(a\) below itself, then the fixed neighbourhood system of the least fixed-point endomap is a subsystem of the fixed neighbourhood system of \(a\).

*Proof.* The pre-fixed-point inequality implies, by leastness, that the least fixed element of \(t\) lies below the element represented by \(a\). Transporting this inequality to approximable maps and using the order characterization of fixed neighbourhood subsystems for finitary projections gives the result. ∎
